package cosmonic:agent@0.4.4;

/// Types shared by `spawn`, `tool-discovery` and `tool-loading`, and the
/// contract every implementer of those interfaces keeps. Function-less:
/// importing it grants nothing. Each power is its own import, so a world
/// grants exactly the ones it names. Unstable: a consumer enables the
/// `agent-spawn` feature (and `agent-run` and `session-status`, whose types
/// these use) to see these items. Rationale and the decisions behind each
/// rule are in `docs/spawn/DESIGN-NOTES.md`.
///
/// "The implementer" is whatever serves those imports: a native host
/// plugin, a policy layer the host installs in front of it, or a test
/// double. "The caller" is the component the import is bound to.
///
/// Caller identity. One identity is used wherever these interfaces bind a
/// caller (approval digests, coalescing, the pending-approval cap, the
/// denial cooldown, the session ceiling, loaded toolsets): the workload
/// incarnation (an id the host mints when the workload is created and keeps
/// across rollouts and restarts, so a workload deleted and recreated under
/// the same name is a different caller) and the component's name within that
/// workload (so sibling components are different callers). Never an
/// instance id, since a rollout replaces instances. Namespace and workload
/// name are display and audit metadata. A policy layer must be installed by
/// the host and pass the original caller through: a guest component placed
/// in front of the implementer would become the caller, and its grants the
/// ceiling.
///
/// Reach. A request's read reach is the union, over the agent and every
/// toolset, of its read grants: volumes, secrets, interfaces the
/// implementer classifies as reading, and the model alias. Its egress reach
/// is the union of allowed hosts and interfaces classified as writing. A
/// read-write volume counts both ways. Tool annotations (`read-only` and the
/// rest in `tool-types`) are publisher claims and never feed reach,
/// admission or the ceiling; see `tool-types` for how hosts treat them.
///
/// Ceiling. A caller's ceiling is two things: its own reach, and its
/// session ceiling (below). A request fits the ceiling when every grant fits
/// inside the caller's own reach, or when each toolset that does not is an
/// exact match for an entry in the session ceiling. Containment is decided
/// on what a grant reaches, not on its name: a volume fits only if the same
/// storage backs one of the caller's mounts, and an allowed host fits only
/// if one of the caller's entries contains it (`*.github.com` contains
/// `api.github.com`, not `github.com`). A grant whose reach the implementer
/// cannot see or compare is beyond the ceiling. Within the ceiling, results
/// flowing up to the caller are not a leak: the caller could have read the
/// same data itself.
///
/// Session ceiling. When a person approves a toolset for a session, the
/// approval adds that toolset to the session's ceiling: a set of entries,
/// each keyed to the caller, the toolset's request digest, and the
/// admission snapshot it was reviewed at (executable digest, allowed hosts,
/// the storage behind each volume and its access, credential bindings,
/// secrets, interface resources, materialized defaults; see `approval`).
/// The one-time grant on the approval is consumed by the first load. A
/// later load of the identical toolset (a rebind after the host unbound it,
/// or a return after it was set aside) is a load within the ceiling and
/// needs no new approval. The rules:
///
/// - Re-checked at each rebind: the implementer resolves the request again
///   and compares it with the entry's snapshot, and checks current policy,
///   in the same step that admits it. Any difference (a new digest, a host,
///   a volume rebound to other storage, a replaced credential binding, a
///   policy change) leaves the toolset outside the ceiling, and the load
///   needs a new approval.
/// - Never widens: an entry admits only its exact snapshot, never something
///   that merely fits inside it. Only a person's approval adds an entry;
///   reusing an entry adds nothing.
/// - Only the person removes: removing a toolset from the session (a
///   person's action in the host's UI, never an import here) drops its
///   entry, so a later load asks again. Setting a toolset aside, unbinding
///   it, a rollback or a failed rollout keep the entry.
/// - Session-scoped: it lives in the session's state, moves with the session
///   between hosts, and ends with the session. Never per agent, never across
///   sessions.
/// - Audited: every load admitted by an entry records the approval it
///   rests on.
///
/// Attenuation. A caller may request more than its ceiling but cannot grant
/// it. The implementer is the sole grantor (by policy, an auditor the host
/// installs, or a person) and returns `needs-approval`. An approval is an
/// explicit declassification (a decision that data may cross a boundary it
/// otherwise could not), bound to a digest over the caller's identity and
/// the request, and to the admission snapshot taken when it was raised.
/// Policy may ask for approval within the ceiling too: within the ceiling,
/// the implementer admits at once only toolsets and agents whose digest is
/// in the operator's catalog or in the session ceiling, and sends any other
/// digest to a grantor, since new code is new risk even when its reach is
/// not.
///
/// Children. A spawned child's reach never exceeds its parent's ceiling.
/// The child agent component's own `grants` must fit inside the parent's
/// own reach, or the spawn is `denied`. A child's toolset beyond the
/// parent's ceiling goes to a grantor; the person's approval adds it to the
/// parent's session ceiling, so the invariant holds after the grant too. A
/// child's own ceiling is its spawned reach plus its own session ceiling,
/// and its session ceiling is bounded by its parent's: a child's load beyond
/// its own ceiling but within its parent's goes to a person (labelled with
/// the child), and one beyond its parent's ceiling is `denied`.
///
/// Authorship. A model can paraphrase anything it read, so redaction is not
/// a security boundary and a child's final reply is a declassification
/// channel by nature. Nothing else the caller sees carries child-authored
/// text: progress, errors and reasons are implementer-authored, and every
/// detail string is diagnostic only. A child's journal is never observable
/// through these interfaces; a person sees it in the host's own view.
///
/// Steerable channels. A child chooses its counts, its finish code, which
/// error a run ends in, whether an outcome exceeds `output.max-bytes`, and
/// its timing. Each is a narrow channel to the caller, minor next to the
/// reply itself. Nothing here closes covert or timing channels, and nothing
/// here blocks prompt injection.
///
/// Toolsets. A toolset is one component, with its own grants, in the
/// agent's own workload, that offers one or more tools: either a tool
/// provider (it exports `cosmonic:agent/tools`) or a typed tool (it exports
/// its own interfaces, whose functions the host offers as tools; see the
/// package README). The implementer lists and dispatches across the
/// toolsets, so an agent never carries a router. A toolset egresses as
/// itself, with its own grants and identity; the calling agent and its
/// session are audit context, never delegated authority. The implementer
/// may offer a tool under an alias that is not its own name and may bound
/// what a call returns, as `tool-types.tool-response` describes.
///
/// Credentials never pass through these interfaces. Models, secrets and
/// credential references are named; the implementer resolves them and keeps
/// values out of every agent's memory. A spawned agent's own component never
/// receives secrets.
///
/// Authority to decide. A spawned agent never holds approval authority: the
/// grantor is a person, or policy a person wrote. An auditor is installed by
/// host policy, never spawned, so the agent it reviews cannot choose its
/// grants, budget or lifetime.
@unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
interface spawn-types {
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use wasi:clocks/types@0.3.0.{duration};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use inference-types.{json};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use run-types.{run-outcome, run-counts};

    /// A component image pinned by content digest, so a moving tag cannot
    /// change what was approved.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record component-ref {
        /// An OCI reference such as `ghcr.io/acme/agents/coder:1.2.0`. For
        /// display; `digest` is authoritative.
        image: string,
        /// An OCI digest, `sha256:<hex>`.
        digest: string,
    }

    /// A named volume mounted into one component.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record volume-mount {
        /// The name of a volume the implementer knows, resolved in the
        /// caller's scope. Never a host path. Two workloads may use one name
        /// for different storage, so the implementer compares the storage,
        /// never the name, and an approval binds the storage it resolved to.
        volume: string,
        /// Where the component sees the volume.
        mount-path: string,
        /// True mounts the volume read-only. Read-write counts toward both
        /// read and egress reach.
        read-only: bool,
    }

    /// An interface granted to one component. The implementer, not the
    /// request, classifies it as reading, writing, or both.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record interface-grant {
        /// Fully qualified interface name with version, such as
        /// `wasi:keyvalue/store@0.2.0`.
        name: string,
        /// Interface-specific configuration, such as a bucket or topic.
        config: list<tuple<string, string>>,
    }

    /// What one component may touch. Empty grants nothing; nothing is
    /// inherited from the caller.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record grants {
        /// Hosts this component may reach.
        allowed-hosts: list<string>,
        /// Volumes mounted into this component.
        volumes: list<volume-mount>,
        /// Interfaces this component may import.
        interfaces: list<interface-grant>,
        /// Secret names the implementer delivers into this component's
        /// memory. Names only, never values. Refused on a spawned agent's own
        /// component, whose memory credentials never enter; use
        /// `credentials` for authenticated egress.
        secrets: list<string>,
        /// Credential references: stable logical names, not secret data.
        /// The implementer resolves each through a credential binding the
        /// operator scoped to this workload, applies it to the component's
        /// outbound requests, and owns those authentication headers. The
        /// value never enters the component. An implementer without a
        /// credential broker treats every reference as beyond the ceiling.
        credentials: list<string>,
    }

    /// A toolset: one component, with its own grants, that offers one or
    /// more tools. Used by `spawn` for a child's toolsets and by
    /// `tool-loading` for a toolset the caller loads.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record toolset-spec {
        /// A DNS label: lowercase letters, digits and `-`, starting and
        /// ending with a letter or digit, at most 32 characters. Unique among
        /// one agent's toolsets. Display metadata and the prefix of a
        /// namespaced alias; never used for routing or authorization, which
        /// follow the toolset's component identity and digest.
        name: string,
        /// A tool provider (exports `cosmonic:agent/tools`) or a typed tool
        /// (exports its own interfaces).
        component: component-ref,
        /// What this toolset component may touch.
        grants: grants,
        /// The functions offered, by their own names; none offers every
        /// function the component has. Trims a large component to what a
        /// job needs, so it spends less of the model's function budget.
        /// A name the component does not have is `invalid-request`.
        functions: option<list<string>>,
        /// The operator's override of how this toolset's tools are offered.
        /// None takes the default: each tool keeps its own name if no alias
        /// in the session has it yet, and is otherwise offered as
        /// `<name>__<tool>`. Some(prefix) offers every tool as the prefix
        /// followed by its own name, so the empty string means the own names
        /// and `"gh__"` means `gh__search`. An alias already taken, or one
        /// that breaks model tool-name rules (only `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, at most
        /// 64 characters), refuses the request with `invalid-request`; the
        /// implementer never renames it. In a caller's request the
        /// implementer refuses it unless its operator lets callers choose
        /// prefixes.
        alias-prefix: option<string>,
    }

    /// What may come back to the caller. Covered by the approval digest.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record output-spec {
        /// JSON Schema that `structured` must satisfy. None means reply
        /// parts only.
        schema: option<json>,
        /// Ceiling on the encoded outcome of one run, in bytes.
        max-bytes: u32,
        /// True returns `structured` only: the implementer empties `reply`
        /// before the caller sees it, and refuses the request with
        /// `invalid-request` when `schema` is none. For reading untrusted
        /// content in quarantine. The implementer does not check that the
        /// schema has no free strings; an approval summary states that only
        /// structured output returns.
        structured-only: bool,
    }

    /// What a child may spend. Covered by the approval digest. None takes
    /// the implementer's default, which policy may cap. The implementer
    /// enforces it, not the child: it refuses a run past `max-runs`, ends a
    /// run at `max-duration`, and meters the child's model calls itself
    /// (it serves the child's `chat`), so token counts are its own.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record budget {
        /// Runs the child may take: the number of `agent.run` calls
        /// accepted.
        max-runs: option<u32>,
        /// Model calls the child may make in one run (`max_turns` in the
        /// OpenAI Agents SDK, the Claude Agent SDK and Goose). Passed to the
        /// child as `run-options.max-steps` and enforced by the implementer's
        /// metering.
        max-steps: option<u32>,
        /// Tokens the child may spend across all runs and model calls: the
        /// sum of `usage.prompt-tokens` and `usage.completion-tokens`.
        /// Cached, cache-write and reasoning tokens are parts of those two
        /// and are not added again.
        max-total-tokens: option<u64>,
        /// Wall-clock budget, measured from spawn. Passing it mid-run ends
        /// the run with an outcome whose finish is `max-duration`.
        max-duration: option<duration>,
    }

    /// Powers a spawned child may receive. Empty grants none: a child never
    /// inherits its caller's powers, and receives a power only when the
    /// caller holds it too.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    flags agent-powers {
        /// The child may call `spawn`, within the operator's tree limits.
        spawn,
        /// The child may call `tool-discovery.list`.
        tool-discovery,
        /// The child may load toolsets into itself, bounded by its parent's
        /// ceiling.
        tool-loading,
    }

    /// Everything needed to start a child agent. Declarative: its declared
    /// toolsets are fixed at spawn; it changes them only through
    /// `tool-loading`, when it holds that power. Construction is lenient;
    /// `spawn` validates and returns `invalid-request`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record spawn-request {
        /// A component targeting `cosmonic:agent/runnable-agent` (it exports
        /// `run`), so the implementer can hand it runs.
        agent: component-ref,
        /// A model alias, as `models.open` takes it, resolved against the
        /// aliases the caller's spawn grant delegates. Counts toward read
        /// reach.
        model-alias: string,
        /// The child's instructions. Caller free text: not covered by the
        /// approval digest and never shown in its summary.
        instructions: string,
        /// What the agent component itself may touch. Must fit inside the
        /// caller's own reach; reach the caller lacks goes in a toolset.
        grants: grants,
        /// The child's declared toolsets, in order (the order assigns
        /// aliases). Names must be unique.
        toolsets: list<toolset-spec>,
        /// What may come back, and how much.
        output: output-spec,
        /// What the child may spend over its lifetime.
        budget: budget,
        /// Powers the child receives.
        powers: agent-powers,
        /// For logs, audit and display, and returned by `spawn.children`.
        /// Never policy input, and not covered by the approval digest.
        labels: list<tuple<string, string>>,
    }

    /// Where a toolset stands in the caller's session.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant toolset-state {
        /// Admitted, and its rollout has not finished: not callable yet.
        loading,
        /// In the model's tool list: its definitions are offered and its
        /// tools are callable.
        offered,
        /// Bound and approved but set aside: out of the model's tool list to
        /// keep it within the function budget. A `load` of the same spec
        /// returns it to `offered` at once, with no approval and no
        /// rollout. Its aliases answer `unknown-tool` with how to bring it
        /// back.
        ready,
        /// A state not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A grantor's decision on a request that needed approval.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant decision {
        /// Granted. Resend the identical request; the grant admits one
        /// request in total, however many waiters shared the approval. For
        /// a toolset, it also adds the toolset to the session ceiling.
        granted,
        /// Denied, with an implementer-authored reason. Identical requests
        /// from the same caller answer `denied` for the host's denial
        /// cooldown and raise no new prompt.
        denied(string),
        /// No decision before the approval expired, or the implementer
        /// withdrew it because its admission snapshot no longer matches. Not
        /// a denial: an identical request raises a new approval.
        expired,
        /// A decision not captured above. Treat as denied.
        other(string),
    }

    /// An earlier admission of the same digest from the same caller, which
    /// makes a new approval a repeat.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record prior-admission {
        /// The id of the approval whose grant admitted it.
        approval-id: string,
        /// How long before this approval was raised it was admitted.
        admitted-ago: duration,
        /// True while the child that admission started is still running.
        still-running: bool,
    }

    /// A request sent to a grantor. The approval is host state: it stays
    /// pending until the grantor decides or it expires, whether or not
    /// anyone holds this handle. Dropping the handle only stops waiting, so
    /// a person who decides after the caller's request has ended is not too
    /// late.
    ///
    /// One logical request, one admission. Identical requests from the same
    /// caller while an approval is pending coalesce onto it (the same id,
    /// one prompt), and every waiter sees the same decision. The grant is
    /// consumed atomically, exactly once, across every waiter and resend:
    /// it admits one child or one load. For a spawn, a resend after the
    /// grant is consumed or expired is a new request with a new approval,
    /// marked as a repeat (`get-repeat-of`) within the repeat window. For a
    /// load, an identical toolset already loaded or loading is returned as
    /// it is, so the losers of a race get the winner's toolset.
    ///
    /// The grant expires: it admits the request only within the operator's
    /// grant lifetime, checked in the same atomic step that consumes it.
    ///
    /// Admission snapshot. Before raising an approval, the implementer
    /// resolves the request into an immutable snapshot: the storage behind
    /// each volume, the credential binding behind each reference (the binding
    /// and what it authenticates as and to, not the secret value, so rotation
    /// within a binding changes nothing), the secret each name resolves to,
    /// the model alias's provider, model and endpoint, the resource and
    /// classification of each interface grant, every executable digest, and
    /// every default the request left open (budget fields that are none, and
    /// the configured operator limits, never live counters). The person-facing
    /// approval UI shows the snapshot and the audit log records it; it never
    /// reaches the caller, whose summary carries at most an opaque, keyed
    /// fingerprint. Consuming the grant resolves the request again and
    /// compares it inside the atomic step; any difference spends the grant
    /// and the resend gets a new approval. While pending, a request whose
    /// names now resolve differently withdraws the stale approval (its
    /// decision becomes `expired`) and raises one new approval. A toolset's
    /// session ceiling entry is keyed to this snapshot.
    ///
    /// After a denial (or `other`), the same digest from the same caller
    /// answers `denied` with no prompt for the host's cooldown (recommended
    /// one hour), keyed by digest and caller, so a rebinding neither lifts
    /// nor restarts it. Each caller may have at most a capped number of
    /// distinct approvals pending (operator-set, at least one, recommended
    /// three); past it a request answers `over-limit` with scope
    /// `pending-approvals` and raises no prompt.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    resource approval {
        /// Implementer-issued id, to correlate with the approval UI and the
        /// audit log.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-id: func() -> string;

        /// `sha256:<hex>` over a domain tag naming the operation and its
        /// version (`cosmonic:agent/spawn-request@3` or
        /// `cosmonic:agent/toolset-spec@3`), a zero byte, the caller's
        /// workload incarnation, a zero byte, the caller's component name, a
        /// zero byte, and the request in canonical JSON. Compare for equality
        /// only.
        ///
        /// It covers exactly the fields the summary shows: the whole
        /// `toolset-spec` for a load; every `spawn-request` field except
        /// `instructions` and `labels` (caller free text) for a spawn.
        ///
        /// Normalization, before digesting and before the ceiling check:
        /// `allowed-hosts` entries are parsed, scheme and host lowercased, an
        /// internationalized host converted to its A-label form, one trailing
        /// dot stripped, and a default port (80 for `http` and `ws`, 443 for
        /// `https` and `wss`) dropped; an entry that does not parse is
        /// `invalid-request`. Set lists (`allowed-hosts`, `secrets`,
        /// `credentials`, `volumes`, `interfaces`, `functions`) drop exact
        /// duplicates and are sorted by the UTF-8 bytes of each element's
        /// canonical JSON; two different elements with one key (two volumes
        /// at one `mount-path`, two interface grants with one `name`) are
        /// `invalid-request`. `toolsets` keeps its order. Names, digests and
        /// paths are compared as given.
        ///
        /// Each WIT value as JSON, serialized by RFC 8785 (JCS): a record is
        /// an object with every field, named as in this WIT; a string, bool
        /// or u32 is itself; u64 and `duration` are strings of decimal digits;
        /// an option is `null` or the value; a list is an array, and
        /// `list<tuple<string, string>>` is an object (a repeated key is
        /// `invalid-request`); flags are a sorted array of set names; a
        /// variant is its case name, or a one-member object `{"case":
        /// payload}`; `json` is embedded as parsed JSON.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-request-digest: func() -> string;

        /// Implementer-authored text for the person who decides: what is
        /// declassified ("results of this agent, schema S, at most N bytes,
        /// return to <caller>"), whether the toolset joins the session
        /// ceiling, and what a load would set aside. Built only from values
        /// the implementer validated; caller free text never appears in it.
        /// A repeat opens by saying so. It never carries resolved
        /// infrastructure detail from the admission snapshot.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-summary: func() -> string;

        /// Some when the same caller had a spawn with this digest admitted
        /// within the repeat window (the operator's; recommended one hour,
        /// extended while that child runs): the most recent such admission.
        /// Only spawns set it.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-repeat-of: func() -> option<prior-admission>;

        /// Resolves when the grantor decides. Dropping the future stops
        /// waiting and does not withdraw the request.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        wait-for-decision: async func() -> decision;
    }

    /// Which implementer limit a request would exceed.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant limit-scope {
        /// Children running at once in one spawn tree (the root session and
        /// every descendant). Clears when one ends. Default 20.
        concurrent-agents,
        /// Nesting depth, where a child of a session a person started is at
        /// depth 1. Does not clear. Default 3.
        depth,
        /// Children spawned in total by one spawn tree, ended ones
        /// included. Does not clear. Unset by default.
        total-agents,
        /// Memory the implementer reserves for a child.
        memory,
        /// Toolset components bound in one agent's workload at once, set
        /// aside ones included. Clears when one is unbound. Default 16.
        loaded-toolsets,
        /// Functions offered to the model at once, when nothing can be set
        /// aside to make room (every offered toolset is pinned, or was
        /// loaded or called within the residency window). The budget comes
        /// from the model binding.
        offered-functions,
        /// Approvals one caller has pending at once. Always set. Clears when
        /// one is decided or expires.
        pending-approvals,
        /// A scope not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// An implementer limit a request would exceed.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record over-limit {
        /// Which limit.
        scope: limit-scope,
        /// The limit's value, where the implementer reports it.
        limit: option<u64>,
    }

    /// Why a spawn started no child, or a load loaded nothing. Either way
    /// nothing ran and no reach was added. Detail strings are
    /// implementer-authored and diagnostic only.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant admission-error {
        /// The operator has not enabled this power on this host, or did not
        /// grant it to this caller. Permanent for this caller: adapt rather
        /// than retry.
        disabled,
        /// A policy or a person refused, or the request reaches beyond what
        /// the caller's position allows (a child's reach beyond its parent's
        /// ceiling). A different request may succeed. Also the answer, with
        /// no prompt, during the cooldown after a person denied the same
        /// request.
        denied(string),
        /// Sent to a grantor. Wait for the decision, then resend the
        /// identical request. Returned at once: a pending approval never
        /// holds the caller's run.
        needs-approval(approval),
        /// An implementer limit would be exceeded.
        over-limit(over-limit),
        /// Malformed: a malformed digest, a duplicate toolset name, a name
        /// already held by a different toolset, an unknown model alias or
        /// function, a component that does not target the right world.
        invalid-request(string),
        /// The implementer could not serve the call, or the rollout failed
        /// and left the caller as it was. Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent. Diagnostic
        /// detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why a run is paused. Implementer-authored.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant blocked-reason {
        /// A tool call or a load waits on a person or the auditor.
        tool-approval,
        /// Policy holds the outcome until a person or the auditor releases it.
        outcome-release,
        /// A reason not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Contentless progress. The implementer coalesces frames and never
    /// blocks on an unread stream.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant progress {
        /// The child is working.
        working,
        /// The run is paused.
        blocked(blocked-reason),
        /// Cancellation was requested and the child has not stopped yet;
        /// `agent.run` answers `busy` until it has.
        cancelling,
        /// Cumulative counters for this run so far, from the implementer's
        /// own metering.
        counts(run-counts),
        /// A frame not captured above. Safe to ignore.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A budget item that can leave a child unable to take another run.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant budget-item {
        /// `budget.max-runs` runs were accepted.
        max-runs,
        /// `budget.max-total-tokens` were spent.
        max-total-tokens,
        /// `budget.max-duration` passed.
        max-duration,
        /// An item not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why a child's run did not start. Nothing reached the child. Not the
    /// `run` export's `start-error`: this one adds what only an implementer
    /// that holds a budget knows, and never says `duplicate`, since the
    /// implementer mints the child's run ids.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant start-error {
        /// A run is in progress, or a cancelled one has not stopped yet.
        busy,
        /// A budget item is spent; the child takes no more runs.
        exhausted(budget-item),
        /// The message cannot be taken. A different message may succeed.
        invalid-input(string),
        /// The child has ended for good (it failed to start, its parent's
        /// session ended, or it stopped) and will never accept another run.
        failed(string),
        /// The run could not be accepted now. Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent. Diagnostic
        /// detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why a child's run failed after it started. Detail strings are the
    /// implementer's, never the child's. The child's own typed errors reach
    /// the caller only as `failed`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant run-error {
        /// The caller dropped the progress stream. Cooperative: effects
        /// caused before the child stopped are not rolled back.
        cancelled,
        /// The outcome failed `output.schema` or exceeded `output.max-bytes`.
        output-rejected(string),
        /// A person or the auditor declined to release the outcome.
        withheld,
        /// The child failed. Details are in the host's view of its session.
        failed(string),
        /// The implementer could not finish serving the run.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent. Diagnostic
        /// detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A child's run that has started: contentless progress, then the
    /// outcome. Unlike the `run` export's stream, the child's text and steps
    /// never reach the caller. The stream closes before the outcome
    /// resolves. Dropping the stream requests cancellation, as for the `run`
    /// export; an outcome already recorded wins the race. The outcome can be
    /// awaited without draining the stream.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    type started-run = tuple<stream<progress>, future<result<run-outcome, run-error>>>;
}
